    "description": "Write a Python function called `most_common_sorted_1` that must return up to 1 most frequent values using deterministic tie sorting. Practice collections module.\nDifficulty: Easy.\nExamples:\n  most_common_sorted_1([1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1]) -> [1]\n  most_common_sorted_1([]) -> []",
    "initialCode": "from collections import Counter\n\ndef most_common_sorted_1(values):\n    pass",
    "solution": "from collections import Counter\n\n# Example 1: canonical function approach\ndef most_common_sorted_1(values):\n    counts = Counter(values)\n    return sorted(counts, key=lambda value: (-counts[value], value))[:1]\n\nprint(most_common_sorted_1([1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1]))  # Expected: [1]\nprint(most_common_sorted_1([]))  # Expected: []\n\n# Example 2: nested helper approach\ndef solve_problem_6913(values):\n    def calculate():\n        counts = Counter(values)\n        return sorted(counts, key=lambda value: (-counts[value], value))[:1]\n    return calculate()\n\nprint(solve_problem_6913([1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1]))\n\n# Example 3: static method approach\nclass Problem6913Solution:\n    @staticmethod\n    def most_common_sorted_1(values):\n        counts = Counter(values)\n        return sorted(counts, key=lambda value: (-counts[value], value))[:1]\n\nprint(Problem6913Solution.most_common_sorted_1([]))\n\n# Example 4: assigned result approach\nresult = most_common_sorted_1([1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1])\nprint(result)\n\n# Example 5: boundary case\nprint(most_common_sorted_1([3, 2, 3, 2]))",
