    "description": "Write a Python function called `csv_row_count_1` that must count CSV data rows and add 1. Practice csv & json.\nDifficulty: Easy.\nExamples:\n  csv_row_count_1(\"a,b\\n1,2\\n3,4\") -> 3\n  csv_row_count_1(\"a\") -> 1",
    "initialCode": "import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ndef csv_row_count_1(text):\n    pass",
    "solution": "import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\n\n# Example 1: canonical function approach\ndef csv_row_count_1(text):\n    rows = list(csv.reader(StringIO(text)))\n    return max(0, len(rows) - 1) + 1\n\nprint(csv_row_count_1(\"a,b\\n1,2\\n3,4\"))  # Expected: 3\nprint(csv_row_count_1(\"a\"))  # Expected: 1\n\n# Example 2: nested helper approach\ndef solve_problem_7023(text):\n    def calculate():\n        rows = list(csv.reader(StringIO(text)))\n        return max(0, len(rows) - 1) + 1\n    return calculate()\n\nprint(solve_problem_7023(\"a,b\\n1,2\\n3,4\"))\n\n# Example 3: static method approach\nclass Problem7023Solution:\n    @staticmethod\n    def csv_row_count_1(text):\n        rows = list(csv.reader(StringIO(text)))\n        return max(0, len(rows) - 1) + 1\n\nprint(Problem7023Solution.csv_row_count_1(\"a\"))\n\n# Example 4: assigned result approach\nresult = csv_row_count_1(\"a,b\\n1,2\\n3,4\")\nprint(result)\n\n# Example 5: boundary case\nprint(csv_row_count_1(\"\"))",
